# Astro Pages and React Islands

## Rendering Strategy

- Prefer **Astro-first** rendering for public pages, SEO content, layouts, and static sections
- Use React islands only when interactivity, client state, or browser-only APIs are required
- Do not turn a static page into a client-heavy React page unless the user experience clearly needs it

## File Responsibilities

- `src/pages/**/*.astro`: route entrypoints, page data wiring, metadata, and high-level composition
- `src/layouts/**/*.astro`: document shell, SEO tags, global wrappers, and cross-page structure
- `src/components/pages/*.jsx`: page-level React sections when Astro-only markup would be too cumbersome
- `src/components/*.jsx`: reusable UI building blocks
- `src/content/**`: editorial content and blog material

## Routing

- Respect Astro file-based routing exactly
- Do not introduce React Router, client-side route state, or SPA assumptions
- Keep route slugs stable when they are public or indexed

## SEO and Medical Content

- Preserve canonical URLs, meta descriptions, headings, and structured data on SEO-sensitive pages
- When editing blog or service pages, avoid weakening semantic structure such as `h1`, section headings, and FAQ markup
- User-facing copy is Russian-first unless the task explicitly introduces another language
- Do not make unsupported medical, legal, or compliance claims

## Hydration Discipline

- Use the lightest viable client directive and avoid hydrating more UI than needed
- Keep browser-only code inside interactive components, not in shared server-rendered modules
- Prefer passing serialized props from Astro pages into React components instead of fetching the same data again on the client

## Layout and Composition

- Keep page composition top-down: page -> section components -> small reusable components
- Reuse existing page wrappers, headers, banners, and CTA blocks before adding new variants
- Extract shared logic before copying the same section pattern across multiple medical pages
